# Summary
A security bug was introduced. The new assumed-role GetObject denial path returns detailed policy-denial messages only after an existing object has been loaded, while the missing-object path remains generic AccessDenied. This creates an object-existence oracle for unauthorized assumed-role callers.
For assumed-role GetObject authorization, missing objects are deliberately mapped to generic AccessDenied by forcing can_discover_missing to false. However, when the object exists, the authorization callback is executed and a denied same-account assumed role can receive the new AssumedRolePolicyAccessDenied error with an AWS-style detailed message such as "because no identity-based policy allows the s3:GetObject action". For a missing key, the response remains the generic "Access Denied". An authenticated assumed-role session without GetObject/ListBucket permission can therefore probe guessed object keys and distinguish existing objects from missing objects by comparing 403 response bodies.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Assumed-role GetObject requests force can_discover_missing=false before object load, intending to hide missing keys.
- [x] Missing object storage errors map to generic ServerError::AccessDenied when can_discover_missing=false.
- [x] Existing objects execute the read authorization callback and same-account assumed roles without GetObject policy get typed AssumedRolePolicyAccessDenied(NoIdentityPolicyAllow).
- [x] HTTP response rendering exposes different client-visible messages for generic AccessDenied versus AssumedRolePolicyAccessDenied while both are 403 AccessDenied.
- [x] A targeted PoC/debugger trace demonstrates the two paths and their attacker-observable differential.
## Report
Validated the object-existence oracle. Direct crash reproduction is not applicable to this authorization/response-body logic flaw; targeted Rust tests completed without crashing. Valgrind validation was attempted but valgrind is not installed in the container. I then used LLDB non-interactively to trace the two divergent paths.

Evidence:
1. Missing-object hiding is explicitly enabled for assumed-role GetObject: crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz/modern.rs:605-609 sets can_discover_missing=false when req.requester.is_assumed_role_session().
2. With can_discover_missing=false, ObjectNotFound maps to generic ServerError::AccessDenied: crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz/acl.rs:1192-1196.
3. For an existing object, same-account assumed role with no matching identity/resource policy reaches AssumedRoleObjectPolicyDecision::Denied(Some(NoIdentityPolicyAllow)): crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz/modern.rs:1000-1005, then becomes ServerError::AssumedRolePolicyAccessDenied at modern.rs:1334-1358.
4. HTTP rendering makes the two 403 AccessDenied responses distinguishable: generic ServerError::AccessDenied renders "Access Denied" at crates/server-http/src/http/response.rs:185-188, while AssumedRolePolicyAccessDenied renders a detailed message at response.rs:189-218.

Targeted PoC tests added in the artifact patch:
- server-core PoC output: "POC existing object denial = AssumedRolePolicyAccessDenied(NoIdentityPolicyAllow); missing object denial = AccessDenied".
- server-http PoC output: "POC generic 403 AccessDenied message: Access Denied" versus "POC detailed 403 assumed-role AccessDenied message: User: arn:aws:sts::111122223333:assumed-role/put-role/put-session is not authorized to perform: s3:GetObject on resource: \"arn:aws:s3:::test-bucket/key\" because no identity-based policy allows the s3:GetObject action".

Debugger evidence: LLDB breakpoint at modern.rs:1003 showed the existing-object path in assumed_role_object_policy_decision with action=GetObject and resource_policy=NoMatch returning NoIdentityPolicyAllow. After continuing, a breakpoint at acl.rs:1194 showed the missing-object path with can_discover_missing=false returning generic AccessDenied. This confirms an unauthorized assumed-role caller can distinguish existing from missing keys by comparing 403 response bodies.

# Resolution
Fixed by commit `768bd24c` (`Remove premature assumed-role authorization support`).

The premature assumed-role authorization implementation was removed rather than patching only the observable response difference. The commit:

- removes the assumed-role-specific identity/resource-policy composition and object-policy decision path from `server-core`;
- removes the assumed-role missing-object discovery branch, typed denial kinds, `AssumedRolePolicyAccessDenied` server error, and detailed HTTP rendering that created the object-existence signal;
- removes the wider set of assumed-role behavioral gates and tests whose behavior had not been established by a shared AWS/local conformance suite;
- introduces the opaque `auth::ConfiguredOrAnonymousAuth` proof, constructed by an exhaustive principal-kind classification in the `auth` crate;
- requires that proof when `server-core::Requester::from_auth` is called, so `server-core` contains no credential-kind authorization branch and cannot receive an unsupported authenticated identity accidentally;
- maps unsupported authenticated identities to the ordinary generic `AccessDenied` response in `server-http`; and
- locks the fail-closed boundary across buffered S3, S3 Control, POST Object, streaming PutObject, and streaming UploadPart adapters.

As a result, an assumed-role session may still be authenticated, but it cannot enter core authorization. Both existing and missing GetObject targets are rejected at the same pre-authorization boundary with generic `AccessDenied`, and the detailed assumed-role denial renderer no longer exists.

Verification completed after the fix:

- `cargo nextest run`: 7,486 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped;
- `cargo nextest run -p server-http unsupported_authenticated_identity_fails_closed_across_core_request_adapters`: passed; and
- `cargo clippy -p auth -p server-core -p server-http --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`: passed.

